                        PicoRV32 SoC
			Grug Huhler

This directory contains a simple mini-SoC that uses the RISC-V (32-bit)
soft core from https://github.com/YosysHQ/picorv32.  File picorv32.v
is from there, unchanged.  It is the core.  There is also associated
software.

The mini-SoC is built and tested on the Tang Nano 9K and 20K FPGA
development boards.  These use FPGAs made by Gowin. They are very
inexpensive, and bread-board friendly.  I bought mine from Amazon in
the USA.

The project uses the Gowin FPGA designer IDE and tool chain.

See https://www.gowinsemi.com/en/ and register for an account.  Login
and look for "Gowin EDA" under Products.  You can use either the
educational version or the standard version.  The former requires no
license.  For the standard version, you must request a license
(currently free of charge) and, I think, renew it annually.  The
educational version is an OK choice.

In my experience, the Windows version of the Gowin tools is easier to
install and get going than the Linux version.

There are separate Gowin project files for the Tang Nano 9K and the 20K,
but many verilog files, including top.v, are shared between the projects.
Conditional compilation is sometimes used.

Of course, the mini-SoC cannot run without software.  Directory c_code
contains example "bare-metal" type software.  Note that the software
build process also creates some verilog files.  This means that, in
general, you must build the software before using the Gowin IDE to
build the FPGA image.

YouTube Videos
--------------

I am an electronics hobbyist, and I document my tinkerings on YouTube
videos.  In fact, this repository supports the YouTube videos more
than the other way around.  I assume that most people find this
repository in the first place because they saw one of my videos.

Check out https://www.youtube.com/@electronics.tinker

There is a playlist for the PicoRV32.

First video about PicoRV32: https://youtu.be/cq7ETOCPIBM.  See this
for some installation and getting started tools.  Be aware that Gowin
sometimes updates the toolchain.  I suggest using the latest.

On Linux (Ubuntu 22.04 tested), I use openFPGALoader for loading the
bitstream file onto the FPGA.  See that first video.  The Gowin
Programmer in the IDE works fine on Windows.

Quick Start?
------------

The git has Verilog files that contain prebuilt software.  Look at
src/global_defs.v to see which board the build is for.  If it is
not your board, you will have to rebuild the software.

For the Tang Nano 9K, you should be able to just build and run the
Verilog.  The Tang Nano 20K is a little more complicated due to its
more flexible clock source.  See below.

But, it is best to read on...  Summary: first build the software with
"cd c_code; make clean; make 9K|20k" and then use the Gowin IDE to
build the verilog bitfile.  The bitfile contains the software in the
form of SRAM initialization files.  There is no bootloader (at this
time).

On Windows, use the Gowin programmer within the IDE.  On Linux, the Gowin
programmer has never worked for me so I use openFPGAloader (which I built
from source).

Connect a terminal emulator to the USB-serial port that the Tang Nano board
provides.  You should see something print when the FPGA starts.

Components
----------

File top.v is the top level module and integrates the SoC's
components which include:

* The RISC-V core (PicoRV32).
* SRAM controller.
* Adjustable amount of SRAM memory. See c_code/Makefile.  Default
* size may be as low as 8192 bytes, but you can increase it.
* Single clock used, speed adjustable via the Gowin rPLL.  See
  c_code/Makefile.
* A small reset controller.
* A UART (wrapper around the simpleuart from
  https://github.com/YosysHQ/picorv32).
* An LED driver that supports reads and writes to the 6 standard LEDs on the
  Tang Nano9K and TangNano20K.
* A 32-bit count-down timer that can be read and written.
  It counts down to zero and stops.
* A Gowin user flash controller implemented two ways:
  - uflash.v uses the Gowin user flash controller soft
    IP block.  Clock speed limit = 50 MHz.
  - uflash_controller.v is a controller that I wrote. Clock
    speed limit = 40 MHz.
  You can use either controller. Just enable one of them using
  the Gowin GUI.  uflash.v is faster, especially for slow
  clock speeds.  I tested both with clocks speeds of 5.4 and
  40.5 MHz (the latter being a slight overclocking for uflash.v).
  Note that only the Tang Nano 9K contains the user flash.
* A controller for the ws2812b "neopixel" RGB LED that is only
  only on the Tang Nano 20K
* Interrupt support.  There is a YouTube video about this.
* An SD card controller that uses SPI mode.  The implementation is a
  hybrid of bit-banging and hardware support.  There is a YouTube
  video about this.  You could adapt the pure bit-banging version to
  any microcontroller, I think.

Both the Verilog and the C code in this project are intended to be very
simple and direct.  There are few integration abstractions.  The hope
is that the project is relatively easy to understand completely, and easy
to add to.  Peripheral modules preset a uniform interface based on the
PicoRV32 standard memory interface bus document at the PicoRV website
mentioned above.

Some Tang Nano Hardare Topics
------------------------------

S1 is the reset button.  Serial port: 115200, no parity, no flow control.
The serial port is provided over the same USB port that powers the FPGA
board and allows it to be programmed.  At least for Linux, the needed
USB serial driver is already present.  I am not sure about Windows.  I
installed no special driver, but may have installed a compatible driver
at some point in the past.

The project requires a 27 MHz input clock to the rPLL.  On the Tang
Nano 9K, this comes from a 27 MHz can oscillator on pin 52.

On the Tang Nano 20K, the clock comes from the external clock
generator on pin 10.  You *MUST* configure the generator to be 27
Mhz. See video: https://youtu.be/I-N0brdlzzk


Software
---------

Directory c_code contains software to run on the SoC.  See the README
there for information on the toolchain.  The software makefile
generates some Verilog files that support parameterization and
conditional compilation of the main Verilog modules.  Some Verilog
configration is done in the software's Makefile.  Look at that
Makefile for how to set the system clock speed and the SRAM memory
size.

In particular, the software build process creates Verilog SRAM block
initialization files.  That's how the software gets onto the FPGA.  I
have not created a serial or USB loader.  You must build the software and
then rebuild the FPGA bitfile when you change the software.

github and branches
-------------------

This project is hosted on github at

https://github.com/grughuhler/picorv32_tang_nano_unified

You will find all needed Gowin projects files and software source files.

Look for tags that indicate releases, usually associated with a
YouTube video.

For older versions, I have used these repositories (and branches on
them):

  https://github.com/grughuhler/picorv32
  https://github.com/grughuhler/picorv32_tang_nano_20k
  
I suggest avoiding these unless trying to replicate particular things from
older videos.



